<h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
<p>Buy when your process is close to how most businesses in your field work and a product already does most of what you need. Build when the way you work is part of what makes you different, when off-the-shelf tools force your team into workarounds every day, or when you need to connect systems in a way no product supports. Most teams end up with a mix: bought software for the standard parts and a small custom layer where it matters.</p>

<h2 id="why-buy-first">Why buying is usually the starting point</h2>
<p>Bought software arrives tested, supported and updated by someone else. Its cost is visible up front in a subscription, and you can usually try it before committing. For accounting, email, a standard CRM, HR and most common business functions, the products available are mature and the way you work is unlikely to be unusual enough to justify building your own.</p>
<p>Buying also lets you move quickly. A team that spends months building something a product could have done in a week has spent its budget on the wrong problem.</p>

<h2 id="signs-to-build">Signs a custom build makes sense</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The workarounds have become the process.</strong> Your team exports to spreadsheets, re-keys data between systems, or keeps parallel records because the tools do not fit. That hidden work is a cost you pay every day.</li>
  <li><strong>Your way of working is an advantage.</strong> If how you quote, schedule, match or serve customers is part of why they choose you, forcing it into a generic product can wear that advantage away.</li>
  <li><strong>Several tools need to act as one.</strong> When the real work spans your CRM, your inventory, your accounting and a partner portal, a small application that joins them may be simpler than any single product.</li>
  <li><strong>You have tried the products.</strong> You have evaluated the leading options with real cases, and each one fails on something essential, not just something preferred.</li>
  <li><strong>The task involves reading or deciding.</strong> Sorting documents, extracting details, matching records or drafting responses are areas where a tool shaped around your data can outperform a general one.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="hidden-costs">The hidden costs on both sides</h2>
<p>Bought software has costs beyond the subscription: per-user pricing that grows with the team, add-ons for features you assumed were included, the time spent configuring it, and the risk that the vendor changes the product or the price later. Getting your data out when you leave can also be harder than getting it in.</p>
<p>Custom software has its own: someone has to host it, secure it, fix it when a connected system changes, and add features as needs evolve. A custom tool with no owner and no budget for upkeep becomes a liability within a year or two. Count that ongoing care when you compare, not just the build.</p>

<h2 id="the-middle-ground">The middle ground</h2>
<p>The choice is rarely all or nothing. Common middle paths include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Configure before you build.</strong> Many products allow custom fields, workflows and automations that close most of the gap.</li>
  <li><strong>Integrate.</strong> Integration tools and the products' own APIs can connect systems without a full application.</li>
  <li><strong>Build a thin layer.</strong> Keep the bought systems as the record and build a small application on top for the part of the work that is unique to you.</li>
  <li><strong>Start with a prototype.</strong> A simple working version used by a few people for a few weeks tells you more than any requirements document.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="an-example">A simple example</h2>
<p>Consider a distributor that quotes custom bundles. Its CRM handles contacts and its accounting system handles invoices, but every quote is assembled in a spreadsheet, checked against stock in a third system and re-typed into the CRM. Buying a different CRM would not fix that, because the gap is between the systems. A small quoting tool that reads stock, applies the pricing rules and writes the finished quote back to the CRM keeps the bought systems in place and removes the re-typing.</p>

<h2 id="questions-to-ask">Questions to ask before you decide</h2>
<ol>
  <li>What exactly goes wrong with the current tools, and how often?</li>
  <li>Have we tested the leading products against our real cases, including the awkward ones?</li>
  <li>Is the way we do this part of what makes us different?</li>
  <li>Who will own a custom tool after it is built, and what will its upkeep involve?</li>
  <li>What data must move between systems, and can it be moved reliably?</li>
  <li>What would we lose if the vendor changed its pricing or product?</li>
  <li>What is the smallest version we could test before committing?</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="where-ai-changes-it">Where AI changes the picture</h2>
<p>AI has lowered the cost of building some kinds of tools, especially ones that read documents, classify requests or draft text. That makes a small custom tool viable in places where it would not have been a few years ago. It has also made many bought products better at the same tasks. So the comparison is worth redoing even if you ruled out building before. Whichever way you go, keep a person reviewing the outputs that matter until the results have earned trust.</p>

<h2 id="decide-in-stages">Decide in stages</h2>
<p>Treat build or buy as a series of smaller decisions rather than one large bet. Map the work, test the best products against it, and if they fall short, build the smallest piece that closes the gap. Measure what changes. If it works, extend it. If it does not, you have spent a little to learn a lot, which is a good trade in either direction.</p>
